<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thomas Mann</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1875">1875</a>–<a href="/years/1955">1955</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Paul Thomas Mann</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paul Thomas Mann was a German novelist, short story writer and essayist, born to a Hanseatic family in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>, who won the 1929 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature. He portrayed his family and class in his first novel, Buddenbrooks (1901), and went on to write Death in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> (1912), The Magic Mountain (1924), the tetralogy Joseph and His Brothers and Doctor Faustus (1947). When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> came to power in 1933 he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, and in 1939 he moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, becoming one of the best-known writers of German literature in exile. He returned to Switzerland in 1952 and died in Zürich in 1955.</p></div>
